{
  "requirement": "A {term}`requirements.references.definition_selection` in a requirements input MUST continue to select requirements and named examples, excluding named definitions. Definitions referenced by selected texts MUST be captured as separate definition inputs, regardless of whether their names match the selection. Sharing a selection's matching name prefix alone MUST NOT make a definition a dependency.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is implemented completely and is tested. I found no unacknowledged gaps.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Selection excludes named definitions.**\n  - `InputSelector::Requirements` resolution in `src/inputs.rs` (`resolve`) skips every `DefinitionKind::Term` before applying `definition_glob::matches`.\n  - An exact name selects exactly that definition, and a name or glob that matches only a named definition fails with \"matches no requirement or example\".\n  - The same exclusion is applied wherever selections are used:\n    - `source_input` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (read-permission checks).\n    - `status_markdown_with_options` and `info_markdown` in `src/commands.rs`.\n    - `update_inputs` in `src/add_input.rs`, which resolves selectors through `resolve`.\n- **Referenced definitions become separate inputs, regardless of name.**\n  - `terms::direct_references` scans the subject text and each input.\n    - For a `Requirements` input it scans the bodies of the selected declarations for `{term}` references.\n    - For outline inputs it scans the text (Rust sources only inside comments).\n    - A `Definition` input counts as a reference to itself.\n  - `terms::dependencies` and `close_dependencies` expand these to a transitive fixed point.\n    - `close_dependencies` carries no `selection` marker. Its transitive expansion is covered by the `dependencies` and `direct_references` markers, so I do not count that as a gap.\n  - Each required definition is captured as its own `definition` input:\n    - `update_inputs` records them in evidence.\n    - `definition_inputs` supplies them to authoring.\n    - `validate_dependencies` requires exactly one such input per required definition, wherever the term is declared.\n- **A shared prefix alone never creates a dependency.** Dependencies come only from actual `{term}` references, never from name matching, so an unreferenced definition under the same prefix is not captured.\n- **Marking.** The `{impl}` markers sit on the functions that carry out the behaviour, including `preprocess_book` in `src/lib.rs`. I found no selection logic that lacks a marker, and no impl code whose behaviour the requirement does not cover.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- **`tests/definitions.rs`, `globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob`.**\n  - The glob `rules.*` selects only `rules.ready`, and the selected text contains no `{definition}` block.\n  - Captured definitions are exactly `glossary.a`, `glossary.b` (transitive) and `rules.used`.\n  - The captured `rules.used` falls inside the glob's prefix and is captured because it is referenced.\n  - The unreferenced `rules.unrelated` shares the prefix and is not captured.\n  - `validate_repository` raises no error mentioning `rules.unrelated`.\n- **`tests/definitions.rs`, `selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions`.**\n  - `matches` is `[rules.case, rules.ready]`, so the example is selected and the unreferenced definition `rules.used` is not.\n  - `add_input` with the exact name `rules.used` fails with \"matches no requirement or example\".\n  - The `status rules.*` and `info rules.*` CLI runs list the requirement and the example but not the definition, and `info rules.used` fails.\n- **`src/authoring/tools.rs`, `requirements_reads_select_requirements_and_examples_but_not_definitions`.** The authoring `habeas` tool's `requirements` form behaves the same way. `requirements.*` matches the example and the requirement, no extra definitions are delivered, and the exact name of a definition is rejected.\n\nTogether these cover the positive case, the exact-name case, the prefix-sharing boundary, the transitive-dependency case, and the failure cases, across the CLI, evidence capture and validation, and authoring paths.",
  "references": "2f245a0a2a79fa55401b14b2ff61f57c0953d41d08e781ce65ef052194505e72",
